    {
      "data": {
        "text": "**Choosing OAuth Provider**\n\nI've decided to proceed with Google as the external OAuth provider for the `start_oauth_login.py` script. It seems like a solid, widely-supported choice for the implementation of an OAuth authorization-code login. This makes implementation and testing a bit easier, as there are many readily available resources.\n\n\n**Initiating Authorization Request**\n\nOkay, I'm now fully focused on step one: constructing the authorization URL for the Google OAuth flow. This URL will be used to redirect the user. I've broken down the necessary parameters like `client_id`, `redirect_uri`, `scope`, and `response_type`. I'll be using the `requests` library to demonstrate this. The next challenge is making sure the redirect URI is properly set up, and that the authorization URL is correctly formatted, to kick off the flow.\n\n\n**Defining Authorization URL**\n\nI'm now focusing on defining Google's authorization endpoint and constructing the URL. I've decided to define placeholder values for `CLIENT_ID` and `REDIRECT_URI`, as these are specific to an application registered with Google, but are needed to create the URL. The `SCOPE` variable will also define permissions requested. I've broken down the construction into steps: defining the endpoint, defining client and redirect URIs, and finally, crafting the authorization URL. My main focus now is on assembling these parts into a valid, illustrative URL that would be written to the `start_oauth_login.py` file.\n\n\n**Simplifying OAuth Flow**\n\nI've decided to concentrate on the essential first step: crafting the authorization URL. The subsequent steps, such as handling redirects and token exchange, are omitted for this demonstration, as they would need a web server. I'm focusing on making a straightforward script to begin the login process. It will solely create and display the authorization URL for user interaction.\n\n\n",
        "time": {
          "end": 1789862151777,
          "start": 1789862147488
        },
        "type": "reasoning"
      },
      "id": "prt_0bc18f5a00019EFtyz9UjQLbNx",
      "message_id": "msg_0bc18f048001VvmZb7wh6HH2tB",
      "session_id": "ses_f43e71052ffeLvXI7ZEpYiUeMm",
      "time_created": 1789862147488,
      "time_updated": 1789862151777
    },
    {
      "data": {
        "callID": "KZhiAjI7b4GppY7i",
        "metadata": {
          "google": {
            "thoughtSignature": ""
          }
        },
        "state": {
          "input": {
            "content": "\nimport os\n\n# Google OAuth 2.0 Endpoints\nAUTHORIZATION_BASE_URL = \"https://accounts.google.com/o/oauth2/v2/auth\"\n\n# Replace with your actual client ID and redirect URI from Google Cloud Console\nCLIENT_ID = os.environ.get(\"GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID\", \"YOUR_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID\")\nREDIRECT_URI = os.environ.get(\"GOOGLE_REDIRECT_URI\", \"http://localhost:8080/callback\")\n\n# Scopes define the permissions your application requests\nSCOPE = \"openid email profile\"\n\ndef start_google_oauth_login():\n    \"\"\"\n    Constructs and prints the Google OAuth authorization URL.\n    The user should open this URL in their browser to initiate the login process.\n    \"\"\"\n    params = {\n        \"client_id\": CLIENT_ID,\n        \"redirect_uri\": REDIRECT_URI,\n        \"response_type\": \"code\",\n        \"scope\": SCOPE,\n        \"access_type\": \"offline\",  # Request a refresh token\n        \"prompt\": \"consent\",      # Force consent screen\n    }\n\n    import urllib.parse\n    query_string = urllib.parse.urlencode(params)\n    authorization_url = f\"{AUTHORIZATION_BASE_URL}?{query_string}\"\n\n    print(f\"Please open the following URL in your browser to start Google OAuth login:\")\n    print(authorization_url)\n    print(\"\\nNote: For a real application, you would need a web server running at\")\n    print(f\"'{REDIRECT_URI}' to handle the redirect and exchange the authorization code for tokens.\")\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    start_google_oauth_login()\n",
            "filePath": "start_oauth_login.py"
          },
